# Practical 7 — Cheat Sheet 🎯

Aim: To create a 3D interactive scene in Godot 4 where two spheres move and rotate continuously and randomly change their colours upon collision, using `Area3D`, collision detection, and dynamic materials.

## 1. Scene tree — exact structure

Main scene (`Main.tscn`)

```
Main (Node3D)
├── Ground (MeshInstance3D)
├── Sphere1 (Area3D)  [Sphere.gd]
│   ├── CollisionShape3D (SphereShape3D)
│   └── MeshInstance3D (SphereMesh)
└── Sphere2 (Area3D)  [Sphere.gd]
    ├── CollisionShape3D (SphereShape3D)
    └── MeshInstance3D (SphereMesh)
```

Save the scene as `Main.tscn`.

Attach scripts:

- `Sphere.gd` → `Sphere1 (Area3D)`
- `Sphere.gd` → `Sphere2 (Area3D)`

Important: Both spheres must have the same child names because the script uses `$MeshInstance3D` and `get_node_or_null("MeshInstance3D")`.

